          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %strong Para construction
            is a variety of strictly related functorial constructions that produce a category of parametric morphisms.
          %p
            In the simplest case, one starts with a monoidal category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            and builds a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Para
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Para}(\mathcal{C})
            whose morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to B
            are pairs
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi P
                  %mo ,
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (P,f)
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo :
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P:\mathcal{C}
            is a
            %em parameter
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi P
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: A \otimes P \to B
            is a morphism in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
          %p
            Such a morphism might be visualised using the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagram"} graphical
            language of monoidal categories (below, left). However, this notation does not emphasise the special role played by
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/machine+learning"} machine learning
            have different semantics; by separating them on two different axes, we obtain a graphical language which is more closely tied to these semantics (below, right).
          %p
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/standard_vs_para.png", :width => "500"}/
          %p This gives us an intuitive way to compose parameterised maps:
          %p
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/para_comp2.gif", :width => "500"}/
          %p The Para construction is of relevance in categorical cybernetics, since controlled processes such as machine learning models, economic agents and Bayesian reasoners are straightforwardly modeled as parametric processes of some kind.
          %p
            Indeed, the notation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Para
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Para}(\mathcal{C})
            was originally introduced in (
            %a{:href => "#FongSpivakTuyeras2019"}> Fong, Spivak and Tuyeras 2019
            ), and then successively refined in (
            %a{:href => "#Gavranovic19"}> Gavranovic 2019
            ) and (
            %a{:href => "#Cruttwell2021"}> Cruttwell et al. 2021
            ) for applications to machine learning. (
            %a{:href => "#Capucci2020"}> Capucci et al. 2020
            ) generalized the construction from monoidal categories to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/actegories"} actegories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Jaz+Myers"} David Jaz Myers
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Matteo+Capucci"} Matteo Capucci
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Myers22"} Myers 2022
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Capucci23"} Capucci 2023
          %p
            Nonetheless, the 1-categorical version of Para already appears e.g. in (
            %a{:href => "#HermidaTennent2012"}> Hermida &amp; Tennent 2012
            ), with precursors in (
            %a{:href => "#Pavlovic1997"}> Pavlovic 1997
            ). Also,
            %a{:href => "#Bakovic2008"} Baković 2008, Thm. 13.2
            introduces Copara (under a different name) in the very general context of actions of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategories"} bicategories
          %h2#definition Definition
          %h3#for_monoidal_categories For monoidal categories
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              For
