                %use{:x => "215.385837", "xlink:href" => "#GwOrDJ2ZXtJK7FnRwC56KBvALfI=-glyph6-1", :y => "90.954675"}
          %p
            %strong (2.)
            Allow for
            = succeed "." do
              %em colax actions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonads"} comonads
            (and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graded+monad"} graded comonads
            more generally), since they are colax actions of the terminal monoidal category; and applying Para to a comonad yields a double categorical version of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coKleisli+category"} coKleisli category
            of the comonad.
          %p
            %strong (3.)
            Allow for “dependent parameters”, i.e. for situation in which the parameter
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            of a parametric morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊙
                  %mi P
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: A \odot P \to B
            actually depends on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: (a:A) \times P(a) \to B
            (notation is suggestive). This makes double categories such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/span"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Span
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Span}(\mathcal{C})
            instances of the Para construction (where the left leg encodes the parameter dependency and the right leg is the parametric morphism itself).
          %p
            %strong (4.)
            Have the construction take place in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+category"} complete
            2-categories, as opposed to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Cat}
          %p
            %strong (5.)
            Internal vs. external parameterisation: given a
            = succeed "-actegory" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℳ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊙
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathcal{C}, \odot)
            %em internal
            to the category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vect"} Vect
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊙
                  %mi P
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : A \odot P \to B
            is a linear map. But often we are interested in morphisms linear only in one variable, for instance, a function
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi P
                    %mo ,
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Vect
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : \mathbf{Set}(P, \mathbf{Vect}(A, B))
            %em external
            parameterisation, one which is captured by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            being an object of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℳ
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}Cat
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℳ
                    %mi Act
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}Act
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+graded+category"} locally graded category
          %h2#in_categorical_cybernetics In categorical cybernetics
          %p
            Para has been developed to model effectively the compositional structure of controlled processes, such as those involving agents. The examples better developed are in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deep+learning"} deep learning
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/game+theory"} game theory
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Capucci2020"} Capucci et al. 2020
          %p
            The idea that agents are parametric functions is quite old. In fact, agents take in an input
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and produce an output
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %em weights
            (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %em parameters
            %em strategies
            of a player in an economic game, the control signal of a controlled ODE or a Markov decision process.
          %p Thus agents are naturally modelled as parametric morphisms.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cayley+graph"} Cayley graph
                of a monoid action (together with its category structure) is a low-dimensional example of a Para construction for actegories.
            %li
              %p
                When the base category is set to be the category of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/optics+%28in+computer+science%29"} optics
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mrow
                          %mi Para
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mrow
                              %mi Optic
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi 𝒞
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Para(\mathbf{Optic(\mathcal{C})})}
                recovers the category of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neural+networks"} neural networks
                defined in (
                %a{:href => "#Capucci2020"}> Capucci et al. 2020
                ).
            %li
              %p
                The “monoidal indeterminates” construction of
                %a{:href => "#HermidaTennent2012"} Hermida and Tennent
                is a Para construction for actegories. Hermida and Tennent consider a symmetric monoidal functor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                      %mo :
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi Σ
                      %mo →
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j:\mathbf{\Sigma}\to \mathcal{C}
                (hence: a symmetric monoidal action of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi Σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{\Sigma}
                on
                = succeed ")" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi j
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}(\mathbf{x}:j)
                and ordinary monoidal category
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒞
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi x
                        %mo :
                        %mi j
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}[\mathbf{x}:j]
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\to Y
                in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi j
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}(\mathbf{x}:j)
                are pairs
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi w
                      %mo ,
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (w,f)
                where
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi w
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w
                is in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi Σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{\Sigma}
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi j
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi w
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:X\otimes j(w)\to Y
                in
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            %li
              %p
                If we quotient
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi Para
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Para}(\mathcal{C})
                to get a 1-category by equating connecting components of 1-cells, this is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semicartesian+monoidal+category"} semicocartesian
                monoidal category. In fact it is the semicocartesian
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflection"} reflection
                of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "#HermidaTennent2012"} Hermida and Tennent
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi CoPara
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{CoPara}(\mathcal{C})
                gives the semicartesian reflection of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            %li
              %p
                In particular, if we start from the category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi FdIsometry
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{FdIsometry}
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite-dimensional+vector+space"} finite dimensional
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+spaces"} Hilbert spaces
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isometry"} isometries
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi CoPara
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi FdIsometry
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{CoPara}(\mathbf{FdIsometry})
                are
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stinespring+factorization+theorem"} Stinespring dilations
                of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+channels"} quantum channels
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+channels"} quantum channels
                (see (
                %a{:href => "#HuotStaton2018"}> Huot and Staton 2018
                )).
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+of+a+monoidal+category"} action of a monoidal category
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lens+%28in+computer+science%29"} lens (in computer science)
          %h2#references References
          %p The Para construction first appears (though not under that name) in:
          %ul
            %li#HermidaTennent2012
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Claudio+Hermida"}> Claudio Hermida
                ,
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Tennent"} Robert Tennent
                = succeed "." do
                  %em Monoidal indeterminates and categories of possible worlds
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.tcs.2012.01.001"} doi:j.tcs.2012.01.001
            %li#Pavlovic1997
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Du%C5%A1ko+Pavlovi%C4%87"}> Duško Pavlović
                ,
                = succeed "." do
                  %em Categorical logic of names and abstraction in action calculi
                %a{:href => "https://www.kestrel.edu/people/pavlovic/papers/clna-MSCS97.pdf"} pdf
            %li#Bakovic2008
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Igor+Bakovi%C4%87"}> Igor Baković
                ,
                = succeed "." do
                  %em Bigroupoid 2-torsors
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://edoc.ub.uni-muenchen.de/9209/1/Bakovic_Igor.pdf"} pdf
          %p The terminology “Para construction” first appears in:
          %ul
            %li#FongSpivakTuyeras2019
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brendan+Fong"}> Brendan Fong
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Spivak"} David Spivak
              = succeed "," do
                %em Backprop as Functor: A compositional perspective on supervised learning
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1711.10455"} arXiv:1711.10455
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/abstract/document/8785665"} LICS’19
          %p It has then be developed for the purposes of machine learning in:
          %ul
            %li#Gavranovic2019
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bruno+Gavranovi%C4%87"}> Bruno Gavranović
                ,
                %em Compositional Deep Learning
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.08292"}> arXiv:1907.08292
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li#Cruttwell2021
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G.S.H.+Cruttwell"}> G.S.H. Cruttwell
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bruno+Gavranovi%C4%87"} Bruno Gavranović
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Neil+Ghani"} Neil Ghani
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Wilson"} Paul Wilson
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fabio+Zanasi"} Fabio Zanasi
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Categorical Foundations of Gradient-Based Learning
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.01931"} arXiv:2103.01931
            %li#Cruttwell2024
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G.S.H.+Cruttwell"}> G.S.H. Cruttwell
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bruno+Gavranovi%C4%87"} Bruno Gavranović
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Neil+Ghani"} Neil Ghani
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Wilson"} Paul Wilson
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fabio+Zanasi"} Fabio Zanasi
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Deep Learning for Parametric Lenses
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.00408"} arXiv:2404.00408
          %p
            and generalized further to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/actegories"} actegories
            for the purposes of categorical cybernetics in:
          %ul
            %li#Capucci2020
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Matteo+Capucci"}> Matteo Capucci
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bruno+Gavranovi%C4%87"} Bruno Gavranović
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jules+Hedges"} Jules Hedges
              = succeed "," do
                %em Towards Foundations of Categorical Cybernetics
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2105.06332.pdf"} arXiv:2015.06332
          %p
            The generalization of the Para construction being developed by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Jaz+Myers"} David Jaz Myers
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Matteo+Capucci"} Matteo Capucci
            is expounded in the following talks:
          %ul
            %li#Myers22
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Jaz+Myers"}> David Jaz Myers
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Para construction as a distributive law
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://bryceclarke.github.io/virtual-double-categories-workshop/slides/david-jaz-myers.pdf"} slides
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zB_ifewP8Yk"} video
            %li#Capucci23
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Matteo+Capucci"}> Matteo Capucci
                ,
                %em Constructing triple categories of cybernetic processes
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://zenodo.org/record/8221550/files/main.pdf"}> slides
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://youtu.be/jYxnUy1vH7Q"} video
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Matteo+Capucci"}> Matteo Capucci
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Para Construction as a Wreath Product
                %a{:href => "CQTS#CapucciJan2024"} talk at
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CQTS"} CQTS
                (Jan 2024) &amp;lbrack;slides:
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Capucci-ParaAsWreathProduct.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p
            A view on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+channels"} quantum channels
            via CoPara:
          %ul
            %li#HuotStaton2018
              Mathieu Huot, Sam Staton,
              %em Universal properties in quantum theory
              (QPL 2018) (
              %a{:href => "https://www.mathstat.dal.ca/qpl2018/papers/QPL_2018_paper_68.pdf"}> pdf
              ).
